      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To assess whether pre-existing psychiatric diagnoses increase the likelihood of transitioning from sub-acute to chronic back pain."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Prospective cohort study."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Men (N = 140) experiencing a first onset of low back pain (LBP) were examined for lifetime psychiatric disorders approximately 8 weeks post pain-onset using the Diagnostic Interview Schedule (DIS-III-R), then re-evaluated at 6 months after pain onset to determine who did or did not progress to pain chronicity."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "OUTCOME MEASURE",
            "content": "Transition to chronic pain and disability was based on 6-month self-report measures of pain intensity and perceived disability."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Men with a pre-pain lifetime diagnosis of major depressive disorder had 5 times greater risk of transitioning to chronic LBP (odds ratio [OR] = 4.99; 95% confidence interval [CI] 1.49-16.76). Increased risk was also associated with a pre-pain lifetime diagnosis of generalized anxiety (OR = 2.45; 95% CI 1.06-5.68), post-traumatic stress (OR = 3.23; 95% CI 1.11-9.44), and with current nicotine dependence (OR = 2.49; 95% CI 1.15-5.40). There were no statistically significant effects for abuse or dependence of alcohol or other psychoactive substances."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "DISCUSSION",
            "content": "Lifetime history of major depression or a major anxiety disorder may represent potential psychosocial \"yellow flags\" for the transition to chronicity in men with first-onset LBP. Screening for lifetime depressive or anxiety disorders may identify individuals at higher risk, who may benefit from referral for more intensive rehabilitation."
          }
        ]
      },
